# SPOILERS. Phantom Island: the normal scenario, from Limbo to the island.
#
# A typical way through the port and the voyage, with why each step is taken and what
# the players know after it. For developers: to see the game as a player would without
# replaying it all by hand, and to reach a point in the story with a real journal and
# notebook (unlike `startover`, which jumps there with both empty).
#
# Run it in the game as a builder (Developer), with nobody else playing:
#
#   batchcommand/interactive scenarios.island   one step at a time: `ss` runs the next
#                                               command, `ss 5` the next five, `ll` shows
#                                               it, `jj <n>` jumps, `qq` stops; you can
#                                               play normally in between
#   batchcommand scenarios.island               the whole of it at once
#
# It starts a new expedition, so the journal and the flags are lost.
#
# Evennia reads every `#` line as a comment that ends the command before it: keep one
# after each command. The comments here aren't shown in the game, only in this file.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PROLOGUE AND ACT 1: THE PORT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
startover yes
# A new expedition: the ship in port, the journal and notebooks empty, and you in Limbo.
begin
# The Society Office, with Mrs. Fairweather.
ask fairweather
# Her greeting, and what to ask her: mission, halcyon, chart, letter.
ask fairweather about mission
# Why: the first thing anyone wants to know is what they're here for.
# Now you know: the Halcyon is lost, a compass with her name turned up in the market,
# east of here, and the island is on the Halcyon's chart. The plan: look at the compass
# first, then sail to the island.
ask her about chart
# Why: the chart is where the island is.
# Now you know: a copy is in your ship's cabin, with the island's latitude and longitude.
# Mrs. Fairweather has never doubted it (she is mistaken; nothing says so yet).
east
# The Market: Silas Crane and his stall.
look compass
# Why: Mrs. Fairweather said to look at it first.
# Now you know (C1): HALCYON on the lid. The ship didn't vanish without a trace.
examine compass
# Why: the description invites a closer look.
# Now you know (C2): coral grown into the hinge. Coral grows only under the sea, so the
# compass lay in the sea for a long time. Notebook: coral.
ask crane about compass
# Why: he claims he found it on a beach. With the coral in the journal, he can't keep
# that up.
# Now you know (C3): he traded for it with fishermen who came from the west. The island
# is somewhere west, far out.
southwest
# The Quay, with Old Barnaby.
ask barnaby about sailing
# Why: before a long voyage, ask an old sailor.
# Now you know: latitude from the noon sun was reliable, east and west were guesswork.
# Your ship has a sea clock. Notebook: latitude, longitude, dead reckoning, sea clocks.
aboard
# The Deck. The ship is moored at the quay.
down
# The Cabin: the chart, the sextant, the chronometer, the guide books.
read chart
# Why: the island's position is on it.
# Now you know: the island at 14 degrees South, 40 degrees West.
get sextant
# Why: the Ship will ask for a noon sight, and the sextant only works on deck.
up
# The Deck.
sail
# Why: what can I order? The crew want a position, latitude and longitude.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ACT 2: AT SEA
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
sail/now to 14 south 40 west
# Why: the chart's position, as the Ship asked for it.
# The voyage, all at once (/now): three weeks due west along 14 degrees South.
# Now you know (C4): the sea is empty where the chart puts the island.
# Notebook: positions (used), latitude sailing.
use sextant
# Why: the Ship asks you to check the chart's two numbers.
# Now you know (C4 again): 14 degrees South, exactly the chart's latitude.
down
# The Cabin.
read chronometer
# Why: the sextant gave noon here; the chronometer gives the time at home.
# Now you know (C4 again): 40 degrees West, the chart's longitude too. You are exactly
# where the chart says, and there is no island. One of the chart's numbers is wrong.
up
# The Deck.
ask ship about latitude
# Why: which of the two numbers to trust?
# Now you know: latitude has been measured well for centuries.
ask ship about longitude
# Now you know: longitude needs a good clock; without one it is only reckoning, a guess.
# So the Halcyon's latitude is probably right and the longitude probably wrong: search
# east or west along 14 degrees South.
look sky
# Why: the Deck view mentions seabirds in the evening sky.
# Now you know (C6): at dusk the seabirds all fly west. Seabirds fly home to land at
# night. With C3 (fishermen from the west): the island is to the west.
sail/now west
# Why: west, along the same latitude, as C3 and C6 both say.
# A day's sail. Late in the day: land!
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AT THE ISLAND
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
use sextant
# Why: to check where the island really is.
# Now you know: 14 degrees South, the chart's latitude to the minute.
down
# The Cabin.
read chronometer
# Now you know (C5): 42 degrees West. The latitude was right, the longitude 2 degrees out:
# the chart put the island in the wrong place. That is why nobody found the Halcyon.
up
# The Deck.
ashore
# Lagoon Beach. Act 3 begins here.
journal
# The journal so far: C1, C2, C3, C4, C6, C5, in the order found.
notebook
# The notebook so far.
